      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the length of segment BC in the right triangles ABC and ABD, we can use the Pythagorean theorem, which states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. \n\nFor triangle ABC, we have:\nBC^2 = AB^2 + AC^2\n\nFor triangle ABD, we have:\nAB^2 = AD^2 + BD^2\n\nSince we know the lengths of some of these sides, we can substitute in the known values:\n\nBC^2 = 37^2 + 16^2\nAB^2 = 19^2 + 16^2\nand\nBC^2 = x^2 + 19^2 (where x is the length of BC)\n\nNow, we can equate the first two equations:\n\n37^2 + 16^2 = 19^2 + 16^2\nAnd we can also equate the last two equations:\n\nx^2 + 19^2 = 37^2 + 16^2\n\nWe can solve the second equation for x:\n\nx^2 + 19^2 = 37^2 + 16^2\nx^2 = 37^2 + 16^2 - 19^2\nx^2 = 37^2 - 2(19)(16)\nx^2 = 37^2 - 2(304)\nx^2 = 1369 - 608\nx^2 = 761\nx = √761\nx ≈ 27.58\n\nTherefore, the length of segment BC is approximately 27.58 units. \n\nThe final answer is: \\boxed{27.58}",
